Practice: For the valid address 172.20.5.4, write C code that prints its class and whether it is private, and give the output.

------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    int a, b, c, d;
    const char *ip = "172.20.5.4";
    sscanf(ip, "%d.%d.%d.%d", &a, &b, &c, &d);
    if (a <= 127) printf("Class A\n");
    else if (a <= 191) printf("Class B\n");
    else if (a <= 223) printf("Class C\n");
    else if (a <= 239) printf("Class D (multicast)\n");
    else printf("Class E (reserved)\n");
    if (a == 10 || (a == 172 && b >= 16 && b <= 31) || (a == 192 && b == 168))
        printf("Private address\n");
    else
        printf("Public address\n");
    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------

Step 1: sscanf splits the address: a = 172, b = 20.
Step 2: 172 is in 128–191, so it is **Class B**.
Step 3: Private Class B range is 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255; b = 20 is between 16 and 31, so it is **private**.

------------------------------------------
Class B
Private address
------------------------------------------

Answer: **Class B, private address**.

